$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-3,1)$ and $B(1,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-2)-f(-3,1) \\\\  & =(1)^{2}(-2)-(-3)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =-2-9 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125726/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125726"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5b96a09c-5b60-5b19-a0bc-209f071260b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "0d6c0e64-5693-5ec2-9c50-1aa4f36c9a72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "08a0877e-0f28-5a48-b3d5-857c5b3c592a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "a53e8e60-c7af-5a56-854b-3c55470c6367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a6e5e699-a1a6-51b6-8408-0db585492ba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48f3c73f-f7de-5a34-b25a-1cea343ba2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125755/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-125755/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-125755/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-125755/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-125755/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-125755/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125755" :question/id #uuid "ed56cc8d-593d-54f6-a792-358c8cadfbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-125755" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=12{x}^{3}\\,i-2\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,3)$ to $(0,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,3)$ and $B(0,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(0,-1)-f(-1,3) \\\\  & =(3(0)^{4}-2(-1))-(3(-1)^{4}-2(3)) \\\\  & =2-(3-6) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125755/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125755"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "f48fd68e-aa7e-58e6-8d3c-eb80eaab1ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "8236274e-89b7-56c7-9d49-624c964f0c27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "070b029d-e047-58f1-b5f9-c9e188765246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "58967ece-31e0-56c0-8017-7d76a93d43ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "66498617-ab75-5a2e-bc39-21a85f914dde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b3f228-f935-51b9-9073-132a5358027a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125751/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-125751/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-125751/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-125751/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" "q-125751/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-125751/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125751" :question/id #uuid "b2bdf0db-aa3a-52d3-a38c-bdb4c54754e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-125751" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}-2x)\\,i+2xy\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(1,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125751/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125751"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125760"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "7e33804a-9584-5e7e-a076-d009db80d9d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "d8f73301-feea-5088-bbfb-d20b0e9d7047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "e35b6e20-149c-59ce-b261-b10fd67c982f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "9d210ead-7d7b-5060-adaf-d41b82a4fd0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection/283bc09f1a097ae8400558511ca45f1013e17c797fdb7407be52694190fd744b" :answer/id #uuid "a6c6c0b5-883f-5d8c-8f0c-6c60a02759fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad18530-a973-585c-ab41-23fbdb98e777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125757/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer-field/choices ["q-125757/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-125757/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-125757/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-125757/selection/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" "q-125757/selection/283bc09f1a097ae8400558511ca45f1013e17c797fdb7407be52694190fd744b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125757" :question/id #uuid "503b3395-8143-5a72-b5e4-55d935227b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-125757" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3y-x)\\,i+3x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3xy-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(3(2)(-2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(3(0)(1)-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-12-2-0 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125757/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125757"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125665"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d6a6d25b-94eb-5c01-b0dc-cb23d6eaacc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d75f62c3-b57d-50ca-a13e-f4989f8f5512" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" :answer/id #uuid "5fa58bcf-4a79-5e98-9db0-755b86a1a524" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection/6f916d391661973b3a5a5637f8b1a58ee0365885b48c933d7fb44b8e52c8db65" :answer/id #uuid "443cb921-464a-547e-9b32-49ed68b2f637" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c66f9ef2-19f7-512b-97d7-2461d0bcecb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "998a9bec-3f9e-588d-b499-03820ab5cdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125791/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-125791/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-125791/selection/4c09ef323dd53803d71b0c6f839afd3fa7c67c7a3c52acd9fb673fd4aafc2a26" "q-125791/selection/6f916d391661973b3a5a5637f8b1a58ee0365885b48c933d7fb44b8e52c8db65" "q-125791/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125791" :question/id #uuid "675bf60b-2ea8-5f31-81ff-792447552869" :question/math-academy-id "q-125791" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{3{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-125791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "09029aa3-c509-5bdc-a7e0-d2b9dd46cc25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "130efee0-bb8a-5e2a-bae5-a926f65e674d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f5bed039-2ea9-5c61-9ed8-a6184811ece3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "89ae9dc7-503a-578a-adba-372cb9f081d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f00b32d7-fbee-5fd6-a187-1b0c5e325a62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2be32b04-ab31-53c5-934e-876fcc3f93f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125723/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-125723/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-125723/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-125723/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-125723/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125723/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125723" :question/id #uuid "45c8de27-87b9-5513-94dc-31f88c6a517a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125723" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125723/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "bfb67808-016b-5105-875b-0b549eb89573" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection/e73192e65a40b73e081d572f2433bcd518a9ba3d484e9a41401cb5344a51c6fc" :answer/id #uuid "bc15d2a3-caa8-5c24-bb3e-56b5dda413f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "7fb63788-54bb-5ea3-b550-01c4d583790b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a538fcbf-b7fb-5142-8053-b5b4560f40e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261" :answer/id #uuid "6936fa00-747b-5403-bb49-6dc2e5cbf38a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7be9ce5-146f-5c8a-a68f-86913fd63be9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125792/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-125792/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-125792/selection/e73192e65a40b73e081d572f2433bcd518a9ba3d484e9a41401cb5344a51c6fc" "q-125792/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125792/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125792/selection/b37d32edd92c5a2b44069c29e35844b5f1ed599f01225cff80557800733db261"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125792" :question/id #uuid "93441b7e-7e4e-593b-a1ec-1fd0fdc6cd0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125792" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨1,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(e,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}⟨1,\\,t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}1+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t(e-1)+1,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,t(e-1)+1⟩\\cdot ⟨e-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}e-1+t(e-1)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(e-1)+e\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}(e-1)}{2}+et]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{2}+e \\\\  & =\\frac{3e-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-125792/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125792"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection/e73192e65a40b73e081d572f2433bcd518a9ba3d484e9a41401cb5344a51c6fc" :answer/id #uuid "ae675542-dddc-504f-b90f-c80d35c8463d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "377ce567-9201-5248-948e-a0349caa85ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7fc1344f-5169-5871-8d26-92fc3608d678" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3982f81c-ec72-5213-b356-2857bbe0a5b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "de5b3f16-3da3-5bc1-8fc3-bee03120a625" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c638ef5-8093-5f9a-b69b-c3921cea46b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125789/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-125789/selection/e73192e65a40b73e081d572f2433bcd518a9ba3d484e9a41401cb5344a51c6fc" "q-125789/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125789/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125789/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-125789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125789" :question/id #uuid "399851be-29c2-55a7-82fe-c50e3f83a1eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125789" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,1⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{1}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the circle, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨1-t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨(1-t)^{2}+{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-125789/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125789"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125636"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9548"] :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9549"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,2)-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =(2(2)^{3}-{1}^{2})-(2(0)^{3}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =16-1+4 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8753"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
